WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
A playground has a corner cut out for a tree.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:06.160
Can we still work out how many paving tiles it needs?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.440
An irregular outline does not stop us measuring.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.810
Let us turn the tricky shape into familiar rectangles.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.964
Our learning goal is to solve practical problems with irregular shapes.

00:00:16.964 --> 00:00:18.524
Success criteria.

00:00:18.524 --> 00:00:22.044
I can split a shape or subtract a missing rectangle.

00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:24.964
I can check every outside length for perimeter.

00:00:24.964 --> 00:00:28.172
I can compare designs using the required measure.

00:00:28.172 --> 00:00:33.452
Build an L shape with square tiles, then slide a pencil along possible dividing lines.

00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:40.572
You can split its area into non-overlapping rectangles, or start with a large rectangle and subtract the missing part.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:44.180
Both methods must cover exactly the same surface.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.820
An L-shaped space fits inside an eight-metre by six-metre rectangle.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.820
A three-metre by two-metre corner is missing.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.729
Predict whether its area is greater or smaller than forty-eight square metres.

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:57.409
Subtract the missing six square metres from forty-eight.

00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:03.409
The L-shaped area is forty-two square metres.

00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:08.249
As a check, split it into an eight-by-four rectangle and a five-by-two rectangle.

00:01:08.249 --> 00:01:11.202
Thirty-two plus ten also gives forty-two.

00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:11.442
A larger design fits inside a ten-metre by eight-metre rectangle, with a four-by-three corner missing.

00:01:11.442 --> 00:01:19.882
Plan the area calculation first.

00:01:19.882 --> 00:01:23.282
Keep the border measurement separate from the surface measurement.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:26.482
The usable area is sixty-eight square metres.

00:01:26.482 --> 00:01:30.762
For its border, trace ten, five, four, three, six and eight metres.

00:01:30.762 --> 00:01:33.482
That perimeter is thirty-six metres.

00:01:33.482 --> 00:01:37.360
Different questions about the same design need different measures.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.560
Using the full eight-by-six rectangle would include the tree corner.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.841
That overcounts six square metres.

00:01:43.841 --> 00:01:49.727
Subtract the missing rectangle, or add two rectangles that cover only the actual playground.

00:01:51.807 --> 00:01:53.807
A six-by-five rectangle has a two-by-two corner removed.

00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:55.447
What area remains?

00:01:55.447 --> 00:01:58.576
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:01.816
Thirty minus four leaves twenty-six square units.

00:02:01.816 --> 00:02:08.636
The missing corner is not part of the surface, so subtract it once, rather than including the full bounding rectangle.

00:02:08.636 --> 00:02:10.916
Choose the measure the problem needs.

00:02:10.916 --> 00:02:16.156
For area, split or subtract rectangles; for perimeter, trace every outside edge.

00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:20.956
Draw a garden on squared paper and explain a paving plan and a fencing plan.

00:02:20.956 --> 00:02:24.356
Check that your answers describe the actual design.

00:02:24.356 --> 00:02:26.891
Reflect on which model helped you and why.
